{
  "text": "Let E be any real inner-product space with its induced norm and with measurable sets given by the Borel σ-algebra of its norm topology. No finite-dimensionality or completeness assumption is imposed. Let μ be any probability measure on E. For every pair of real numbers r,s satisfying r ≥ 0 and s > 0, and every u,y ∈ E, set m = (r+s)⁻¹(r u+s y). Then the following equality of measures on E holds:\n\nTilt(Tilt(μ, x ↦ −(r/2)‖x−u‖²), x ↦ −(s/2)‖x−y‖²) = Tilt(μ, x ↦ −((r+s)/2)‖x−m‖²).\n\nHere, for a measure ν and real-valued function f, Tilt(ν,f) means ν.withDensity(x ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(exp(f(x))/Zν(f))), where Zν(f) = ∫ exp(f(z)) dν(z). Thus the second tilt is normalized relative to the measure obtained after the first tilt. The real integral is totalized to zero when its integrand is not integrable; division by zero is totalized to zero, so a zero normalizer gives the zero tilted measure. ENNReal.ofReal converts a nonnegative real number into an extended nonnegative real number and sends negative real numbers to zero. Scalar products and reciprocals in m are real scalar operations. Although the reciprocal convention includes 0⁻¹ = 0, the stated assumptions ensure r+s > 0.",
